{"name":"Neon Carrot","hex":"#ff9832","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"neon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The chemical element (symbol Ne) with an atomic number of 10. The second of the noble gases, it is a colourless, odorless inert gas.\n A form or sample of the element.","A form or sample of the element."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That resembles a neon light; extremely bright; fluorescent"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/neon"},{"term":"carrot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vegetable with a nutritious, juicy, sweet root that is often orange in colour, Daucus carota, family Apiaceae, especially the subspecies sativus.","A shade of orange similar to the flesh of most carrots (also called carrot orange)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat (an animal pelt) with a solution of mercuric nitrate as part of felt manufacture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carrot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Neon","blocks":[{"text":"Neon is a chemical element; it has the symbol Ne and the atomic number 10. It is the second noble gas in the periodic table. Neon is a colorless, odorless, inert monatomic gas under standard conditions, with approximately two-thirds the density of air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Neon_discharge_tube.jpg/330px-Neon_discharge_tube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neon","wikidata":"Q654"},{"title":"Carrot","blocks":[{"text":"The carrot is a root vegetable in the umbellifer family Apiaceae. Carrots are typically orange in colour due to their beta-carotene content, which makes them a rich source of vitamin A. Heirloom variants may be white, yellow, red, purple, or black. They are biennial plants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Vegetable-Carrot-Bundle-wStalks.jpg/330px-Vegetable-Carrot-Bundle-wStalks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carrot","wikidata":"Q81"},{"title":"Shades of orange","blocks":[{"text":"In optics, orange has a wavelength between approximately 585 and 620 nm and a hue of 30° in HSV color space. In the RGB color space it is a secondary color numerically halfway between gamma-compressed red and yellow, as can be seen in the RGB color wheel. The complementary color of orange is azure. Orange pigments are largely in the ochre or cadmium families, and absorb mostly blue light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Shades_of_Orange.svg/330px-Shades_of_Orange.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_orange","wikidata":"Q19847194"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/NeTube.jpg/500px-NeTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/NeTube.jpg/1280px-NeTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/NeTube.jpg/500px-NeTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Neon gas-discharge lamps forming neon's element symbol","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANeTube.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG/500px-Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG/1280px-Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG/500px-Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The first evidence for isotopes of a stable element was provided in 1913 by experiments on neon plasma. In the bottom right corner of J. J. Thomson's photographic plate are the separate impact marks for the two isotopes neon-20 and neon-22.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiscovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg/500px-Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg/1280px-Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg/500px-Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The emission spectrum of neon shows individual wavelengths of light contributing to its perceived color when heated.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVisible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Ne-water_clathrate.png/500px-Ne-water_clathrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Ne-water_clathrate.png/500px-Ne-water_clathrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Crystal structure of Ne clathrate hydrate[38]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANe-water_clathrate.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/FLORIST_%28neon_sign%29.jpg/500px-FLORIST_%28neon_sign%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/FLORIST_%28neon_sign%29.jp